              <a href="#faq" class="flex items-baseline gap-3 py-2.5 border-t border-gray-100 text-[15px] text-gray-700 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline"><span class="w-6 shrink-0 text-gray-400 font-semibold">11.</span><span>FAQ</span></a>
        </nav>
      </details>
    </div>

    <!-- Body -->
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 pb-16 lg:pb-24">
      <div class="prose prose-lg prose-blue max-w-none text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
        <p class="text-2xl font-medium text-gray-900 mb-8 italic">
          Ask ten contractors which is better and you will get a split vote and a few strong opinions. The honest answer is that the flagship drills and impacts from each brand are close enough that most users could not tell them apart on a job. Where the brands actually differ is in battery systems, warranty terms, the specialty tools in each lineup and the way they are sold. Those are the things to decide on, because once you own three batteries, switching costs real money.
        </p>

        <TopPick />

        <div id="short-answer" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            1. The Short Answer
          </h2>

          <p>
            Choose <strong>Milwaukee</strong> if you work in the trades, especially plumbing, electrical or mechanical, or if you want the widest range of specialty tools on one battery. Its M18 and M12 lineups go deep into press tools, cable tools, pipe threaders, grease guns and drain cleaners, and its five-year tool warranty is longer than DeWalt's standard coverage.
          </p>

          <p>
            Choose <strong>DeWalt</strong> if you do general construction, carpentry or remodeling, or if you value easy availability and frequent bundle deals. DeWalt's 20V MAX tools are sold at more retailers, its FLEXVOLT line powers big saws that compete with corded tools, and its kits regularly go on sale with free batteries or bonus tools. Its 90-day satisfaction guarantee and first-year free service are useful extras.
          </p>

          <p>
            For a homeowner who needs a drill, an impact driver, a circular saw and a few more tools over a decade, either brand is a sound choice and you will not regret it. If you only need occasional household tools, consider whether a budget platform like Ryobi serves you better; our <a href="/ryobi-tools-review/">Ryobi tools review</a> covers where it holds up and where it does not.
          </p>

          <p>
            Whichever you pick, buy into one platform and stay with it. The real money in cordless tools is in batteries and chargers, and splitting across two brands doubles that cost while halving the flexibility of each pack you own.
          </p>

          <div class="overflow-hidden border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl my-8">
            <div class="overflow-x-auto">
              <table class="w-full text-left">
                <thead class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-200">
