                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Standard tool warranty</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">5 years limited on most M18/M12 tools</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">3 years limited</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Extras</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Tool tracking via ONE-KEY</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">90-day money back, 1 year free service</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Parent company</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Techtronic Industries</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Stanley Black and Decker</td>
                  </tr>
                </tbody>
              </table>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div id="ownership" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            2. Who Owns Each Brand
          </h2>

          <p>
            Milwaukee Electric Tool began in Wisconsin in the 1920s and has been owned since 2005 by Techtronic Industries, or TTI, a Hong Kong based company. TTI also owns or licenses Ryobi, AEG, Hart, and floor care brands including Hoover. Milwaukee is TTI's professional brand, and it is run with a separate engineering organization from Ryobi.
          </p>

          <p>
            DeWalt dates back to 1924 and is owned by Stanley Black and Decker, the American company that also owns Craftsman, Black and Decker, Porter-Cable, Stanley and Irwin. DeWalt is the professional brand in that family, and like Milwaukee it is engineered separately from the consumer brands under the same parent.
          </p>

          <p>
            Both brands manufacture in several countries, including China, Mexico, Vietnam and the United States, depending on the tool. Each assembles some products domestically from global parts. Country of origin varies by model and can change over time, so check the box if it matters to you rather than assuming based on the brand.
          </p>

          <p>
            Ownership matters for one practical reason: sister brands do not share batteries. Ryobi and Milwaukee are both TTI, and Craftsman and DeWalt are both Stanley Black and Decker, but none of those packs fit across brands without third-party adapters. Shared ownership does not mean shared platforms.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="platforms" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            3. Platforms and Tool Tiers
          </h2>

          <p>
            <strong>Milwaukee M18</strong> is the main 18-volt system, with hundreds of tools covering drilling, fastening, cutting, lighting, plumbing, electrical, automotive and outdoor equipment. <strong>M12</strong> is Milwaukee's compact 12-volt line and is unusually deep, with tools such as ratchets, PEX expanders, inspection cameras, small press tools and right-angle drills that many tradespeople carry for close work. <strong>MX FUEL</strong> is a separate heavy-equipment battery system for things like concrete cut-off saws, breakers and light towers.
          </p>

          <p>
            Within M18, Milwaukee sells tools in tiers. Basic brushed tools sit at the bottom, compact brushless tools in the middle, and <strong>FUEL</strong> tools at the top with the most powerful brushless motors and electronics. Many trades buyers go straight to FUEL for drills, impacts and saws.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>DeWalt 20V MAX</strong> is the main platform. The 20V label refers to the maximum initial voltage without load; the nominal voltage is 18 volts, the same as Milwaukee M18, so neither is more powerful on paper. DeWalt tiers its tools as standard 20V MAX, the compact <strong>ATOMIC</strong> line, and the premium brushless <strong>XR</strong> line. <strong>FLEXVOLT</strong> batteries switch between 20 and 60 volts depending on the tool, which powers DeWalt's largest cordless saws, grinders and outdoor tools while still fitting 20V MAX tools.
          </p>

          <p>
            DeWalt also has a <strong>12V MAX XTREME</strong> subcompact line. It is good and growing, but it is not as broad as Milwaukee M12, which is one of Milwaukee's clearest advantages. If you value a deep compact lineup for tight spaces, that tips the scale toward red.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-3">VOLTAGE MYTH</p>
            <p class="text-xl font-bold text-white mb-3">20V MAX and M18 are the same voltage class.</p>
            <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed m-0">DeWalt advertises maximum initial voltage, Milwaukee advertises nominal voltage. Both are five-cell 18-volt lithium systems. Compare tools on torque, speed and the motor tier, not on the number printed on the battery.</p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div id="batteries" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            4. Batteries Compared
          </h2>

          <p>
            Both brands sell compact packs around 2.0 amp-hours for light tools and higher-capacity packs from about 5.0 to 12.0 amp-hours for saws, grinders and outdoor equipment. Capacity determines runtime. The cell technology and the number of cells in parallel determine how much current a pack can deliver, which matters for high-draw tools.
          </p>

          <p>
            Milwaukee's <strong>HIGH OUTPUT</strong> RedLithium packs and its newer <strong>FORGE</strong> packs use higher-performance cells aimed at demanding tools. DeWalt answered with <strong>POWERSTACK</strong> packs, which use stacked pouch cells instead of cylindrical cells to deliver strong output in a smaller, lighter pack. Owners of both commonly report that these premium packs make a noticeable difference in heavy cutting and in compact drills and impacts.
          </p>

          <p>
            DeWalt's FLEXVOLT is the bigger structural difference. One battery runs both 20V MAX tools and 60V MAX tools, so a framer can run a big worm-drive style circular saw and a drill from the same pack. Milwaukee covers similar ground with high-output M18 packs and FUEL tools, and its separate MX FUEL line takes the very heaviest equipment.
          </p>

          <p>
            Battery warranties are typically shorter than tool warranties. Milwaukee commonly covers many RedLithium packs for three years, and DeWalt covers its packs under its three-year limited warranty, but specific packs and promotions vary, so check the current terms when you buy. Third-party adapters that let one brand's battery run the other's tools exist, but they are not supported by either brand and can affect warranty coverage.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-4">Battery Basics</p>
            <div class="grid grid-cols-2 md:grid-cols-4 gap-4 text-center">
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-white">18V</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Nominal voltage of both M18 and 20V MAX</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-[#38BDF8]">2-12 Ah</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Typical capacity range across both lineups</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-white">60V</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">FLEXVOLT high-voltage mode for big DeWalt tools</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-[#38BDF8]">0</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Packs that fit across brands without an adapter</p>
              </div>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div id="core-tools" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. Drills, Impacts and Saws
          </h2>

          <p>
            The flagship drills and impact drivers from both brands are among the best cordless tools on the market. Milwaukee's M18 FUEL drill and impact driver and DeWalt's 20V MAX XR equivalents trade the lead with every new generation. Published torque figures leapfrog each other, and differences between current flagships are small enough that ergonomics and features usually matter more than raw output.
          </p>

          <p>
            Pick each up in the store if you can. DeWalt drills tend to feel a little lighter in the front, and many users like the grip. Milwaukee's FUEL impacts are known for their drive control modes, which let you limit speed or torque to avoid snapping small screws. DeWalt's impacts have similar precision modes on the XR line. Both brands include LED lights, belt clips and bit holders on most models.
          </p>

          <p>
            In saws, DeWalt's FLEXVOLT circular, miter and table saws are a standout for anyone who wants cordless tools that cut like corded ones. Milwaukee's M18 FUEL circular saws and its miter and table saws are strong competitors. DeWalt has a longer history in jobsite table and miter saws, and many finish carpenters stay yellow for that reason.
          </p>

          <p>
            Impact wrenches are a closer contest than many expect. Milwaukee's M18 FUEL high-torque impact wrenches are a favorite among mechanics, and DeWalt's 20V MAX XR high-torque models are strong rivals. For automotive work, both remove lug nuts easily, but always finish wheels by hand with a calibrated wrench from our <a href="/best-torque-wrench/">best torque wrench guide</a>, since an impact can easily overshoot the specification.
          </p>

          <p>
            For hand-tool work alongside either platform, a budget mechanic's set often makes more sense than branded sockets. Our <a href="/tekton-tools-review/">Tekton tools review</a> covers one of the best values in sockets and wrenches.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="specialty" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            6. Specialty and Outdoor Tools
          </h2>

          <p>
            This is where Milwaukee pulls ahead for many trades. Its M18 and M12 lines include press tools for copper and PEX, pipe threaders, drain cleaners, band saws, cable crimpers and cutters, knockout tools, grease guns and dozens of automotive tools. Plumbers and electricians often choose Milwaukee because no other brand covers as many of their daily tools on one battery.
          </p>

          <p>
            DeWalt is strong in carpentry and general construction specialties: nailers in several gauges, routers, planers, track saws, concrete tools and a large selection of jobsite saws. Its 20V MAX framing nailers and finish nailers are popular with builders, and FLEXVOLT powers heavy tools like large grinders and core drills.
          </p>

          <p>
            In outdoor power equipment, both brands offer string trimmers, blowers, chainsaws, hedge trimmers and mowers. Milwaukee's M18 FUEL outdoor tools, including its QUIK-LOK attachment system, are aimed at landscapers. DeWalt's 20V and 60V outdoor line is solid for homeowners. Neither is the strongest outdoor brand overall; dedicated platforms like EGO lead in mowers and blowers.
          </p>

          <p>
            Lighting and accessories are well covered by both. Milwaukee's lights, radios and heated jackets are popular, and DeWalt has a broad lineup too. Both brands offer modular storage: Milwaukee's PACKOUT and DeWalt's ToughSystem. These systems are another reason people stick with one color, since boxes and organizers from each connect only to their own system.
          </p>

          <p>
            Measuring tools are another useful category. DeWalt's battery-powered green laser levels run on its 12V and 20V packs, which is convenient on a jobsite; see our <a href="/best-laser-level/">laser level guide</a> for how they compare with other brands.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="warranty" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            7. Warranty and Service
          </h2>

          <p>
            Milwaukee covers most M18 and M12 power tools with a five-year limited warranty, longer than DeWalt's standard three years. Some product categories, such as certain outdoor tools, MX FUEL equipment and instruments, carry different terms, and batteries typically have their own shorter coverage. Registering the tool and keeping the receipt makes claims simpler.
          </p>

          <p>
            DeWalt offers a three-year limited warranty on most power tools, plus one year of free service for routine maintenance and a 90-day money back guarantee on many products when bought new. That first-year service and the satisfaction guarantee are useful for buyers who want to try a tool and return it if it is not right.
          </p>

          <p>
            Both brands run networks of authorized service centers, and both have factory service for mail-in repairs. Turnaround times vary by region and season. Owners in the trades commonly report that local availability of a service center matters as much as the written warranty, since a tool sitting in a box for weeks costs money.
          </p>

          <p>
            For homeowners, warranty length is less important than it looks. Light household use rarely wears out a premium drill in five years. For contractors who run tools daily, Milwaukee's longer coverage is a real advantage and one of the reasons it has gained ground in commercial trades.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="price" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            8. Price and Promotions
          </h2>

          <p>
            At regular price, comparable tiers cost about the same. A premium brushless drill and impact driver kit with two batteries and a charger typically runs roughly $250 to $400 at the time of writing from either brand, and bare tools roughly $100 to $250. Entry-level kits from both are cheaper, and flagship FUEL or XR tools sit at the top of the range.
          </p>

          <p>
            Promotions change the math. DeWalt kits regularly appear with bonus batteries or free tools at many retailers, and holiday sales often bring deep discounts. Milwaukee runs frequent battery-and-tool bundle offers, especially at Home Depot, where buying a starter battery kit can include a free bare tool. Patient buyers can save a large share of list price with either brand.
          </p>

          <p>
            Availability differs. DeWalt is sold at Home Depot, Lowe's, Amazon, Walmart, Ace and many independent dealers. Milwaukee is sold mainly through Home Depot in the United States and through professional distributors and specialty dealers. If you prefer to shop at Lowe's, that alone may push you toward DeWalt.
          </p>

          <p>
            Beware of tools from unauthorized sellers. Gray-market and counterfeit tools and batteries exist for both brands, and they may not carry warranty coverage. Buy from authorized retailers or the brand's listed dealers, especially for batteries, which carry a real safety risk if counterfeit.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="verdict" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            9. Who Should Buy Which
          </h2>

          <p>
            <strong>Plumbers, electricians and HVAC technicians</strong> are best served by Milwaukee. The breadth of trade-specific tools on M18 and M12 is unmatched, the compact M12 line is excellent for tight work, and the five-year warranty suits daily use.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Framers, carpenters and remodelers</strong> can go either way, but DeWalt has an edge with FLEXVOLT saws, a strong lineup of nailers and jobsite saws, and wide retail availability. Many general contractors run DeWalt for these reasons.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Mechanics and automotive enthusiasts</strong> tend to prefer Milwaukee, thanks to its M12 ratchets, compact impacts and FUEL high-torque wrenches. DeWalt's automotive range is growing, but Milwaukee has led in this space for years.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Homeowners</strong> should base the choice on where they shop, the deals available and the feel of the drill in hand. Either will last for years of household projects. If you already own a few batteries from one brand, the answer is the brand you already have. If you need only a drill and a few basics for occasional jobs, look at budget platforms before paying professional prices.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-3">THE RULE</p>
            <p class="text-xl font-bold text-white mb-3">Your second tool decides your brand.</p>
            <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed m-0">Before buying, list the next five tools you expect to need. Pick the platform that offers all of them in the tier you want. That list matters more than which drill wins this year's spec comparison.</p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div id="mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            10. Common Mistakes to Avoid
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Comparing 20V to 18V as if they differ</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                DeWalt's 20V MAX and Milwaukee's M18 are both 18-volt nominal systems. The 20V figure is a maximum initial voltage with no load. Judging power by that number leads buyers to the wrong conclusion. Compare published torque, speed and motor tier instead, and try the tools in hand.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Buying a drill before choosing a platform</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                The first tool sets the battery you will be buying into for years. List the next several tools you expect to need and make sure the platform offers each one. Choosing based on a single drill deal often means paying for a second set of batteries later.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Mixing brands to chase deals</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A cheap bare tool from the other brand is not cheap if you then need batteries and a charger to run it. Splitting platforms doubles your battery cost and reduces the flexibility of each pack. Unless a tool is truly unique to one brand, stick to the platform you have.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Relying on battery adapters</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Third-party adapters that let one brand's batteries run another brand's tools are unsupported by either maker. They can bypass protections built into the original pairing, may affect warranty coverage, and add bulk. For occasional light use they may be tolerable, but they are not a substitute for a consistent platform.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Buying from unauthorized sellers</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Counterfeit and gray-market batteries and tools exist for both brands. They may not carry warranty coverage and, in the case of batteries, can pose a real fire risk. Buy from authorized retailers and dealers, check packaging carefully, and be wary of prices far below the usual range.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            11. Frequently Asked Questions
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can I use a Milwaukee battery in a DeWalt tool?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Not directly. The battery connections and shapes are different, and neither brand makes an adapter for the other. Third-party adapters exist but are not supported by either company and may affect your warranty. The practical answer is to pick one platform and build your collection around it, rather than trying to share batteries across brands.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is Milwaukee or DeWalt more durable?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Both brands' professional tiers are built for daily jobsite use, and long-term durability is close. Milwaukee backs most M18 and M12 tools with a five-year limited warranty, while DeWalt offers three years plus a year of free service. Owner reports for both are strong. Care, battery maintenance and the tool tier you choose matter more than the color.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Does the same company make Milwaukee and Ryobi?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Both are part of Techtronic Industries, the Hong Kong based group that also owns several other tool and floor care brands. Milwaukee is the professional line and Ryobi the consumer line, with separate engineering and different battery systems. Ryobi ONE+ batteries do not fit Milwaukee M18 tools, and the reverse is also true.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is FLEXVOLT worth it for a homeowner?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Usually not. FLEXVOLT packs are larger, heavier and more expensive than standard 20V MAX packs. They make sense if you plan to run DeWalt's 60V saws, grinders or outdoor tools. For drills, impacts and light saws, standard 20V MAX packs are plenty. You can always add a FLEXVOLT pack later if you buy a 60V tool.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Which brand has better deals?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Both run frequent promotions. DeWalt kits often come with bonus batteries or free tools, and they are on sale at more retailers. Milwaukee runs regular battery-and-tool bundles, especially at Home Depot. Holiday periods, Father's Day and spring tool events bring the best prices for both. Patient buyers rarely need to pay full list price.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is M12 or 12V MAX XTREME better for compact tools?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Milwaukee M12 has the broader lineup, with many specialty tools for plumbing, electrical and automotive work that DeWalt's 12V line does not yet match. DeWalt's 12V MAX XTREME is well made and growing. If you need compact tools for tight spaces and specialty tasks, M12 is the stronger platform right now.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Where are Milwaukee and DeWalt tools made?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Both brands manufacture in several countries, including China, Mexico, Vietnam and the United States, with some tools assembled domestically from global parts. Origin varies by model and can change over time. Check the packaging or product listing for the specific tool if country of manufacture matters to you, rather than assuming based on brand.
              </p>
            </div>

          </div>
        </div>

